the Swin Transformer had its reign so far.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:09.040
Then let’s follow the paper and see:&nbsp;
What is it that ConvNets have that is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.960
precious and we should keep even in the era&nbsp;
where Transformers take over everything?

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.480
Which steps do the authors take to&nbsp;
“modernize” ConvNets into ConvNeXts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.360
and what are the associated performance gains?
What can we learn from all of this?

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.600
Feel free to jump around in the video to&nbsp;
watch the parts you are most interested in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.640
Also note that some of the concepts we&nbsp;
touch upon, we will explain briefly. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:39.520
others are left a bit unexplained – as&nbsp;
they would deserve a video on their own.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.680
So, it’s ConvNets versus Transformers today.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.880
The authors introduce the paper by&nbsp;
praising ConvNets – Ms. Coffee Bean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.680
how else, their agenda is to resurrect&nbsp;
the idea of convolutional networks!

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.200
So, the authors start of course, by mentioning how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.680
the deep learning renaissance&nbsp;
was driven by ConvNets in 2012:&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.280
The introduction of AlexNet established the&nbsp;
“ImageNet moment” in computer vision and visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:13.280
recognition turned from feature engineering to&nbsp;
designing ConvNet architectures that learn their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:19.360
own features like: VGGNet, Inceptions, ResNet,&nbsp;
DenseNet, MobileNet, EfficientNet, RegNet ...

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:26.240
What do all these networks have in common? Well,&nbsp;
the convolution: a sliding window that processes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:31.280
the visual input in the same manner. So, the&nbsp;
parameters are shared between image locations.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.800
This is a big plus when bounded by computation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.200
Convolutions also induce translation&nbsp;
equivariance. Are you not sure what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:43.200
this means and why the authors used the&nbsp;
word equivariance and not invariance?

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.480
Well, then let Ms. Coffee Bean’s past&nbsp;
self explain this in 60 seconds to you:

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.720
We have an interesting question on our Quiz,
where the majority is wrong. It’s about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.160
convolutions and the difference&nbsp;
between invariance and equivariance.&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.440
Translational or shift invariance&nbsp;
means that irrespectively of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.280
position of a cat, the output of the&nbsp;
convolution operation is the same

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.360
Which it isn’t since the feature map&nbsp;
still reflects the original position.&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.120
So this is actually equivariance because&nbsp;
when the position of the cat changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.000
the output changes in the&nbsp;
same way, reflecting shift.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.120
And with the sliding kernel,&nbsp;
this is exactly what happens.&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.480
So convolutions are not shift&nbsp;
invariant, but equivariant.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.480
If you got this wrong, it’s okay because&nbsp;
these words are used interchangeably in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.280
language (except of highly mathematical contexts).

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:30.640
Please mind that the question&nbsp;
asks about the convolution only.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.280
It was the next day’s trick question&nbsp;
inquiring about the CNN as a whole.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.920
While convolutional layers do not make the&nbsp;
CNN shift invariant but only equivariant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.200
it takes spatial pooling layers&nbsp;
to make it approximately shift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:48.320
invariant by pushing information that was&nbsp;
far away, into approximately the same spot.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.000
Okay, we got it. Convolutions are great. But what&nbsp;
about Transformers? Well, after the introduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:00.720
of Transformers for text processing in 2017, the&nbsp;
transformer actually had some applications on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:07.920
image generation or image-text understanding;&nbsp;
applications which went widely unnoticed.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:13.040
So unnoticed, that today’s paper (like almost&nbsp;
any other paper) does not mention this.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:18.080
The transformer architecture got properly&nbsp;
noticed by the computer vision community when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:24.400
of course, when it was proven to advance&nbsp;
the state-of-the-art on ImageNet.

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:30.080
Okay, then: What is so cool about transformers?&nbsp;
First: The transformer as we know it from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:36.480
Natural Language Processing (or short NLP) is&nbsp;
more-or-less usable out-of-the-box for images.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.840
Exception is, we cite: “the initial “patchify”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:45.920
layer, which splits an image into a sequence&nbsp;
of patches” of for example 16 by 16 pixels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:51.280
and there we have it: the ViT!
Watch our previous videos on this for context.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:57.520
ViT’s scaling laws allows to increase the model&nbsp;
size and the size of the training data and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:04.240
overcomes ResNets. But ViT’s problems are just&nbsp;
around the corner: without the inductive bias&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:09.840
introduced by sliding windows, the solution&nbsp;
space of transformers is larger than for CNNs.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:14.400
Just imagine: A CNN, by construction&nbsp;
cannot converge to anything that is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.080
translational equivariant.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.440
But a Transformer very well can because,&nbsp;
it misses this guiding bias. This is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.800
we mean when we say the solution&nbsp;
space of Transformers is larger.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:29.120
The architecture is more flexible&nbsp;
because the solution space is larger.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:33.360
Therefore, in the case where there is&nbsp;
little training data to find the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:38.960
solution in this bigger space, transformers&nbsp;
are dependent on a lot of training tricks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.960
like heavy data augmentation,&nbsp;
as we have seen with DeiT.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.680
But the biggest problem that transformers&nbsp;
have is everything but ImageNet scenarios:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.960
So, cases where the images are high resolution&nbsp;
and the prediction must be dense: In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:58.880
patchifying into 16 by 16 pixels might be too&nbsp;
coarse to make right pixel-level predictions.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:04.400
But if we were to make the patches smaller, this&nbsp;
would quadratically increase the sequence length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:08.240
because we would need more of&nbsp;
them to cover the same surface.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.000
And we know that when we increase&nbsp;
the sequence length for transformers,&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.440
the compute and memory footprint&nbsp;
increases again quadratically.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:20.560
To overcome this, Hierarchical&nbsp;
Transformers such as the Swin Transformer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:26.640
introduce sliding windows again, which makes them&nbsp;
resemble very much with ConvNets in this aspect.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.240
Noting this, the authors cannot help&nbsp;
but ask themselves, what if they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.440
morph a ConvNet towards a Swin Transformer?

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:37.600
What would we see from this&nbsp;
interpolation to answer the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:43.840
we cite: “How do design decisions in&nbsp;
Transformers impact ConvNet’s performance?”

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:47.200
And imagine, the authors wanted to&nbsp;
know the answer to this so badly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.280
that they run the according experiments&nbsp;
and write a paper about this!

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:56.000
Then let’s follow their steps of modernizing&nbsp;
ConvNets to make them stylish enough for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:01.520
2020s. Since the style of the 2020s&nbsp;
is definitely given by transformers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:05.600
we mean that the authors make&nbsp;
ConvNets more transformer-like.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:12.560
Step 1: Take a ResNet50. The authors also&nbsp;
have experiments with the larger ResNet-200,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.880
but if you want to know about that, read&nbsp;
the paper, more specifically: Appendix C.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:21.280
You should anyway read the paper&nbsp;
yourself and should not trust a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:26.000
PhD student and her Coffee Bean to tell you&nbsp;
everything there is to know about a paper.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:26.720
Or should you?

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:33.760
Okay, we have taken a ResNet50. Now what about&nbsp;
it? Well first, and above all, the authors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:39.360
train it with similar training techniques&nbsp;
as vision Transformers are trained with:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:46.080
They use the AdamW optimizer, use more epochs,&nbsp;
heavy data augmentation, regularization.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.320
All of this increases the performance of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:56.000
ResNet-50 from 76.1% to 78.8%&nbsp;
on ImageNet Top1 Accuracy.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:01.760
So wow, lesson learned: I will not forget about&nbsp;
these ingredients when baking my CNN-based models.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:06.720
The second thing they modernize after they are&nbsp;
done with including the training techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:13.120
is something the authors call the “Macro&nbsp;
Design” of the ResNet. For the stage ratio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:16.800
they adjust the number of blocks in&nbsp;
each stage and improve model accuracy.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:21.360
Then they make the sliding windows in the&nbsp;
ResNet behave more like the patches in vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:26.320
transformers: So with a larger kernel size and&nbsp;
with a stride such that the sliding window does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:32.160
not overlap, this looks already much more like&nbsp;
the non-overlapping patches in the transformer.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.320
This again increases performance,&nbsp;
if only by a little bit.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:44.080
The third stage adopts the ideas that ResNeXts had&nbsp;
introduced before, namely the idea of Inception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:48.720
(if you are old enough to remember that) of&nbsp;
splitting, transforming, and merging information.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:53.200
The keyword here is depthwise&nbsp;
convolutions – a special case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:57.440
grouped convolutions – where the number&nbsp;
of groups equals the number of channels.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.120
Important to note (and here we&nbsp;
cite from the paper is, that):&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:07.680
“depthwise convolution is similar to the weighted&nbsp;
sum operation in self-attention, which operates on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:14.080
a per-channel basis, i.e., [that is] only mixing&nbsp;
information in the spatial dimension.” End quote.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:17.440
And depthwise convolutions, having&nbsp;
reduced the number of FLOPs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:21.680
also reduce the accuracy, but by&nbsp;
simultaneously widening the network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:26.000
the performance is substantially&nbsp;
improved. It’s time for the fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:31.200
stage of the whole improvement process,
which is the so-called “inverted bottlenecks”.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:38.080
We find it a little bit misleading here that&nbsp;
the authors say, that “One important design in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:42.720
every Transformer block is that it creates&nbsp;
an inverted bottleneck, i.e. [that is],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:48.240
the hidden dimension of the MLP block is&nbsp;
four times wider than the input dimension.”

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.200
Hopefully we do not get something wrong here,&nbsp;

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:57.360
but this is the case for every Swin Transformer in&nbsp;
particular, not for every Transformer in general.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:02.000
Because yes, in Swin Transformers that work&nbsp;
hierarchically, the outputs of 4 blocks get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:06.000
concatenated such that in the end, the&nbsp;
hidden dimension increases 4 times.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:11.200
But in any case, they want&nbsp;
to copy this idea by using a:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:16.720
we quote “inverted bottleneck design with&nbsp;
an expansion ratio of 4 used in ConvNets”.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:22.000
And hurray! This increases&nbsp;
performance too, if only by a little.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:26.560
The fifth stage introduces larger kernel sizes.&nbsp;

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:32.160
This tries to equal the power of vision&nbsp;
transformers that have a global receptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:38.320
field and “see” the image at once through&nbsp;
self-attention that spans the whole image.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:44.000
But Swin Transformers limit the self-attention&nbsp;
window, so if the authors increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.880
the ResNets window size, then they&nbsp;
should reach the same compromise.

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:52.800
This results in an increase&nbsp;
in performance of … ahm …&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:57.840
nothing. But it’s clear that small&nbsp;
kernel sizes hurt the performance.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:03.600
And finally, the sixth and final stage&nbsp;
comes with micro design choices, such as:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:07.120
Replacing the ReLU (Rectified&nbsp;
Linear Unit) activation function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:12.480
with GELUs (Gaussian Error Linear&nbsp;
Unit) used in BERT or GPT-2 or ViT.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:16.000
They are using the activation&nbsp;
functions less often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.360
as transformers also do (in the MLP block only).

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:24.560
And with the same motivation, to do everything&nbsp;
more transformer style, the authors also use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:29.680
fewer normalization layers because transformers&nbsp;
use them less often than the ResNet as well.

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:35.200
They are replacing Batch Normalization with Layer&nbsp;
Normalization. Short reminder: In BatchNorm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:40.160
a mini batch of data that has multiple&nbsp;
examples and features associated to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:45.920
the normalization happens across to the batch&nbsp;
/ sample dimension. The Batch normalisation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:51.760
effect is that the magnitude of the features&nbsp;
is similar across data samples in the batch.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:57.520
LayerNorm normalizes across the feature&nbsp;
dimension such that per data sample,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:00.160
the features have around the same magnitude.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:04.640
Historically, LayerNorm was something that&nbsp;
worked better for recurrent neural networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:07.920
whereas BatchNorm was mostly used in ConvNets.

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:12.240
And, surprise! All these micro&nbsp;
modifications have a tremendous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:17.920
impact on performance and by using separate&nbsp;
downsampling layers, this enhanced ResNet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:25.920
achieves an accuracy of 82.0% ImageNet Top1&nbsp;
Accuracy, exceeding the Swin Transformer!

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.280
And these results are comparable, because the Swin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:34.080
Transformer plays in the same league&nbsp;
in terms of computation and size.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:38.880
If you’re curious to know how this&nbsp;
fares in the big model league,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.640
you just have to look at&nbsp;
the grey bars in this Figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:47.280
where they start from a ResNet 200 and add&nbsp;
all these steps with very similar gains.

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:50.560
Okay, cool. We have seen&nbsp;
steady performance increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:52.880
and really impressive numbers and results.&nbsp;

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:59.040
But what are the lessons here with respect to&nbsp;
our ConvNet versus Transformer boxing match?

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:02.480
So, the first lesson here is&nbsp;
that: Not only transformers,&nbsp;

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:08.400
but modernized ConvNets scale too in terms&nbsp;
of size and data – We have not discussed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:13.120
the data aspect, check out the additional&nbsp;
experiments with more data in the paper.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:18.640
The second lesson is related to the tasks:&nbsp;
ConvNeXts can compete in object detection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:22.240
and semantic segmentation with the&nbsp;
Swin Transformer, which we remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:24.480
was a hierarchical version of ViT.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:28.560
Maybe you are wondering what exactly&nbsp;
the novelty of this paper is.&nbsp;

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:35.200
Well, we cite: “It is also worth noting that&nbsp;
none of the design options discussed thus far is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:40.480
novel—they have all been researched separately,&nbsp;
but not collectively, over the last decade.”

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:43.680
And while the authors here are&nbsp;
genuine convolution advocates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:47.280
by reading this paper we must ask ourselves:

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:52.800
What if the bigger lesson of this paper&nbsp;
applies to other architectures, too? What if,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:58.240
while everyone in NLP suddenly jumped onto&nbsp;
the next stage dominated by transformers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:04.080
forgot to stepwise improve RNNs or their Tree-RNNs&nbsp;
or everything that is not fashionable anymore.

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:06.880
So far, we thought that self-attention and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:11.600
way that Transformers process information in&nbsp;
parallel highways is the recipe for success.

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:16.640
But this paper suggests that&nbsp;
it is rather the many seemingly&nbsp;

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:21.440
tiny architecture hyperparameters and&nbsp;
not the architecture itself that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:23.840
tweak the way to state-of-the-art.

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:29.520
Another thing we wonder, and this is maybe&nbsp;
something only the authors can answer, is whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:35.280
the experiments were really conducted in the order&nbsp;
presented in the paper and in this figure here:

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.960
It seems more plausible that these&nbsp;
design choices were added in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:43.440
possible orders and configurations and that&nbsp;
the authors picked and presented everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:45.840
where improvement over improvement can be seen.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:51.520
Don’t get us wrong, we do not criticise the paper&nbsp;
with this. Ms. Coffee Bean just wants to warn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:57.760
me that science is not always this&nbsp;
straightforward ascending line to the finish.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:02.400
And coming back to the contribution and&nbsp;
to the novelty of this paper: Even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:08.640
no new design option was presented, it is the&nbsp;
art of putting everything together or rather:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:15.200
the power of brute-forcing every available option,&nbsp;
that makes this paper bear the fruits it does.

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:20.240
And maybe a tiny critique that almost&nbsp;
any paper in machine learning deserves:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:26.400
Let’s not forget that papers do the&nbsp;
comparisons they like and suit them best.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:30.720
Ms. Coffee Bean will leave you to this&nbsp;
tweet, also linked in the description below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:37.040
where one of the ViT authors points out that&nbsp;
ConvNeXts were compared only to plain ViT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:40.640
while Swin and ConvNexts had heavy augmentation.

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:46.720
Judge for yourself, but also do not&nbsp;
forget to count the number of times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:49.680
you took the baselines you liked the most.

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:53.200
Ms. Coffee Bean, stop teasing our&nbsp;
audience, let’s rather talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:57.680
how we reached 10k subscribers this&nbsp;
Christmas. Celebration video, when?

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:01.440
Ms. Coffee Bean, Ms. Coffee Bean, come&nbsp;
back! Don’t leave us hanging here!

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:09.840
Okay, bye!

